  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Lahu\"\nsubtitle: \"Myanmar\"\nregion: \"Southeast Asia\"\ntags: [ethnography, southeast-asia]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | The Lahu, an ethnic group of Southeast Asia |\n| Where | China (Yunnan), Myanmar (Shan State), Thailand, and Vietnam |\n| How many | About 720,000 in China; also in Myanmar, Thailand, and Vietnam |\n| Language | Lahu, a Tibeto-Burman language with seven tones and subject-object-verb order |\n| Religion | Traditional polytheism, Buddhism, and Christianity |\n| Known for | Color-coded subgroups (Black, Red, White, Yellow Lahu) named after traditional dress colors · Indigo-dyed appliquéd cotton textiles and clothing · Silver ornaments and jewelry · Multiple Latin-script alphabets developed by missionaries |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Lahu are an ethnic group spread across Yunnan, Myanmar, Thailand, and Vietnam. Their most distinctive feature is a subgroup system based on the traditional colors of their clothing: Black Lahu, Red Lahu, White Lahu, Yellow Lahu, and Lahu Shehleh. They speak a Tibeto-Burman language and practice a mix of traditional religion, Buddhism, and Christianity. Their material culture centers on textiles, silver ornaments, and bamboo crafts.\n\n## Material culture\n\nLahu material culture emphasizes textiles and silver, with indigo-dyed appliquéd cotton appearing in most garments and silver dominating personal ornament.\n\n### Textile & pattern traditions\n\nLahu textiles are built from indigo-dyed woven cotton and appliqué, with polychrome bands and fine stitching in red, blue, lilac, cream, and orange.\n\n- **Indigo-dyed appliqué cloth** — Woven cotton cloth dyed dark with indigo and decorated with appliqué applied directly to the ground, used for jackets and bags.\n- **Woman's tube skirt** (*phasin*) — Assembled from three to five unequal horizontal sections of tabby cotton with paired warps, topped with cream bands and broken by polychrome stripes on red or dark blue grounds.\n- **Embroidered and appliquéd cotton** — Coats and caps combining embroidery and appliqué in cotton and wool, fastened with large metal buttons.\n- **Woollen belt weaving** — Men's belts made as woollen bands finished with fringe.\n- **Cloth-and-bamboo wall hangings** — Decorative hangings combining fabric with bamboo, including images such as elephants.\n\n### Clothing & dress\n\nLahu dress is organized by subgroup color, with indigo-dyed appliquéd jackets, coats, skirts, and belts forming the core of recorded clothing.\n\n- **Indigo jacket** — Men's and women's jackets made of indigo-dyed woven cotton with appliqué, fastened with large silver or metal buttons or buckles.\n- **Woman's tube skirt** (*phasin*) — Lower garment assembled from horizontal banded sections of cotton in cream, red, blue, and lilac with applied red lines on dark ground.\n- **Cotton coat** — Women's coat worn with silver brooches, sometimes embroidered and appliquéd.\n- **Boy's cap** — Children's headwear combining cotton and wool with both appliqué and embroidery.\n- **Man's woollen belt** — Fringed band completing the recorded male outfit.\n\n### Architecture\n\nNo information available.\n\n### Ceramics, metalwork & everyday objects\n\nEveryday objects are made from local plant and textile materials, with bamboo and cotton appliqué predominating; pottery and metal tools are not recorded.\n\n- **Bamboo quiver** — Hunting equipment made of bamboo, the only recorded hunting gear.\n- **Bamboo wall hangings** — Decorative hangings combining cloth and bamboo, including elephant imagery.\n- **Appliquéd cotton bag** — Carrying gear decorated with appliqué using the same technique as garments.\n- **Appliquéd passport holder** — Modern travel object showing appliqué technique applied to twentieth-century use, closed with nylon zip.\n\n### Jewelry & body adornment\n\nSilver dominates Lahu ornament, appearing as neck rings, bracelets, earrings, and brooches; beadwork and glass beads also occur.\n\n- **Silver neck ornaments** — Rings worn around the neck, with at least two examples recorded.\n- **Silver earrings** — Women's pairs of silver earrings in the collection.\n- **Silver bracelets** — Women's single and paired silver bracelets.\n- **Silver brooch** — Ornament worn with cotton coats and catalogued as part of the outfit.\n- **Glass bead necklace** — Woman's necklace of glass beads representing beadwork traditions.\n\n## Music & performance\n\nNo direct information on instruments, songs, or performance occasions; Christian Lahu villages show musical neologisms in the language.\n\n## Dance & theatre\n\nNo information available.\n\n## Festivals & rituals\n\nLahu ritual practice centers on naming and spirit protection, with birth names incorporating gender and zodiac day markers.\n\n- **Birth naming system** — Two-syllable names combine a gender marker with information on the zodiac day of birth; boys born on Ox day are called Zanu, girls Nanu.\n- **Spirit protection gatha** (*gatha*) — Buddhist verbal formulas requested from forest monks around 1930–1940 to protect against ghosts and demons, showing traditional spirit concerns addressed through Buddhism.\n- **Surname imposition** — The Lahu historically used only given names until the Chinese government assigned surnames; about 90% now bear Lee or Zhang.\n\n## Foodways\n\nNo information available.\n\n## Oral tradition & literature\n\nThree Latin-script alphabets exist for Lahu, developed by Protestant and Catholic missionaries and by the Chinese government, with extensive documentation in Bible translation and linguistic corpora.\n\n- **Protestant alphabet** — Started in 1906 by American missionaries for Black Lahu in Myanmar; first book published 1908, tone marks added 1917, revised 1950 and 1962; remains the main alphabet in Myanmar and Thailand.\n- **Catholic alphabet** — Developed by Catholic missionaries in Burma in the 1930s, differing mainly in finals; survives among Lahu Catholics in Myanmar.\n- **Pinyin-based alphabet** — Begun in 1953 and approved in March 1957, marking tones with letters l, d, q, r, t, f after the syllable; official script in China, revived in 1989 after disuse during the Cultural Revolution.\n- **Lahu Bible** — Bible translation introducing neologisms in hygiene, music, and education alongside loanwords from English, Latin, and Greek.\n- **English-Lahu Lexicon** — James A. Matisoff's dictionary and collection of Lahu texts from 1960s Thailand, part of language documentation projects.\n\n## Language & religion\n\nLahu is a Tibeto-Burman language with seven tones, subject-object-verb order, and no syllable-closing consonants; traditional religion is polytheistic, with Buddhism widespread in China and Christianity established in Myanmar and spreading.\n\n- **Black Lahu** — The standard dialect in China and lingua franca among Thailand's hill tribes, based on traditional folk taxonomy.\n- **Yellow Lahu** — A newer cluster originating in messianic movements, with speech notably divergent from Black Lahu; refugees built an orthography in 1982.\n- **Lahu Shi** — A dialect notably divergent from other varieties, also arising from messianic movements.\n- **Traditional polytheism** — The folk religion of the Lahu before Buddhism and Christianity.\n- **Buddhist conversion** — Buddhism was introduced in the late 17th century and became widespread, particularly among Lahu in China.\n\n## Glossary\n\n- *Lahu Shi* — Yellow Lahu, named for traditional dress color\n- *Lahu Shehleh* — Fifth Lahu subgroup, named after subgroup color classification\n- *phasin* — Woman's tube skirt made of banded cotton sections\n- *gatha* — Buddhist verbal formula for protection from spirits\n- *Zanu* — Boy's name for those born on Ox day in the zodiac\n- *Nanu* — Girl's name for those born on Ox day in the zodiac\n- *Protestant alphabet* — Latin-script orthography begun in 1906 for Lahu by missionaries\n- *Catholic alphabet* — Latin-script orthography developed by Catholic missionaries in Burma in the 1930s\n- *pinyin-based alphabet* — Official script for Lahu in China, approved 1957, marking tones with l, d, q, r, t, f\n- *appliqué* — Decorative technique of stitching fabric pieces onto a cloth ground\n- *tabby* — Plain-weave cotton fabric\n- *polychrome* — Decorated in many colors\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- Bradley, David. *Lahu dialects*. Oriental monograph series #23. Canberra: Faculty of Asian Studies, Australian National University, 1979.\n- Matisoff, James A. *English-Lahu Lexicon*. Berkeley: University of California Press, 2006.\n- Phạm Huy. *Một phần chân dung: dân tộc La Hủ (nhật ký điền dã)*. Lai Châu: Sở văn hóa thông tin Lai Châu, 1997.\n- James A. Matisoff Collection of Lahu language documentation, Computational Resource for South Asian Languages (CoRSAL) archive; and Matisoff, *Window onto a Vanished World: Lahu texts from Thailand in the 1960's*.\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lahu_people\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lahu_language\n- https://folkways.si.edu/search?query=Myanmar\n- British Museum online collection: https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection\n\n",
